          "type": "text",
          "text": "I need help planning a database migration. We run a multi-tenant SaaS on PostgreSQL 11 on EC2 and want to move to PostgreSQL 15 on a managed service. The primary is around 2.1 TB, we average about 1.2k writes per second during business hours, and we have eight services talking to it. The highest risk tables are orders, invoices, subscriptions, and an append-only audit log. We have to preserve tenant isolation, keep RPO near zero, and keep the customer-facing cutover under 30 seconds if possible. We can tolerate some lag on internal reporting but not on billing or order placement. Can you outline a migration strategy that is realistic rather than generic?",
          "metadata": {
            "database_engine": "PostgreSQL",
            "source_version": "11",
            "target_version": "15",
            "database_size_gb": 2100,
            "services": 8
          }
        }
      ],
      "timestamp": "2026-04-14T16:00:00Z",
      "input_tokens": 780,
      "output_tokens": 0,
      "cumulative_tokens": 780
    },
    {
      "turn_id": 1,
      "parent_turn_id": 0,
      "role": "assistant",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Yes. For this size and write rate, I would avoid a big-bang export/import plan. The practical shape is: 1) prepare the target cluster and compatibility checks, 2) reduce application/database drift before migration, 3) seed the target with a base copy, 4) keep it current with logical replication or CDC, 5) run repeated validation rehearsals, 6) do a tightly controlled cutover with rollback gates. Concretely:\n\nPhase 1: inventory everything that mutates state. That includes background jobs, admin scripts, cron workers, and any service that writes outside the main ORM path. Hidden writers are what usually break near-zero-RPO claims.\n\nPhase 2: make the source safer to migrate. Freeze schema churn except for migration-related changes, clean up long-running transactions, reduce superuser-only extensions, and decide whether you need version-hop testing for PostgreSQL 11 to 15 behavior changes.\n\nPhase 3: initialize a target cluster sized for catch-up, not just steady state. Restore a base snapshot there, then begin continuous change capture.\n\nPhase 4: dual-read for validation, not dual-write for correctness. Dual-write raises reconciliation complexity fast. Let one system remain the source of truth until the cutover.\n\nPhase 5: rehearse cutover against production-like traffic. You need timing for connection draining, replica lag convergence, sequence alignment, and cache invalidation.\n\nPhase 6: cut over in a short write freeze window, switch traffic in layers, verify critical paths first, then resume normal writes.\n\nIf you want, next I can compare logical replication, managed CDC tools, and blue-green style cutover against your specific constraints.",
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "timestamp": "2026-04-14T16:00:24Z",
      "input_tokens": 0,
      "output_tokens": 1050,
